WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.443
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:08.781
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:16.064
Unit fifteen: Global Food Security. Today you will describe past expectations with the future in the past.

4
00:00:16.364 --> 00:00:19.086
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.405
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.599
Food insecurity.

7
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:31.509
Food insecurity means not having reliable access to enough safe and nutritious food.

8
00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:34.964
Food insecurity rises when prices jump.

9
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:39.015
Your turn. Say: Food insecurity.

10
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:43.297
Food insecurity.

11
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.597
Agricultural trade.

12
00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:51.265
Agricultural trade means the buying and selling of farm products between countries.

13
00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.755
Agricultural trade carries rice across the world.

14
00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.385
Your turn. Say: Agricultural trade.

15
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:03.012
Agricultural trade.

16
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:04.681
Crop yield.

17
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:09.494
Crop yield means the amount of food grown on a given area of land.

18
00:01:10.083 --> 00:01:12.164
Better seeds raised the crop yield.

19
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.205
Your turn. Say: Crop yield.

20
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:18.940
Crop yield.

21
00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:20.766
Famine relief.

22
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:26.257
Famine relief means emergency food and help sent to people facing mass hunger.

23
00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.326
Famine relief reached the region too late.

24
00:01:29.917 --> 00:01:33.080
Your turn. Say: Famine relief.

25
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:37.190
Famine relief.

26
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.810
Sustainable agriculture.

27
00:01:40.817 --> 00:01:46.311
Sustainable agriculture means farming that protects soil, water and future harvests.

28
00:01:46.917 --> 00:01:49.938
Sustainable agriculture keeps the soil healthy.

29
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.108
Your turn. Say: Sustainable agriculture.

30
00:01:56.967 --> 00:01:58.850
Sustainable agriculture.

31
00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:00.931
Food supply chain.

32
00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:06.470
Food supply chain means all the steps that bring food from farms to tables.

33
00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:10.081
A broken food supply chain empties the shops.

34
00:02:10.683 --> 00:02:14.334
Your turn. Say: Food supply chain.

35
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.898
Food supply chain.

36
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:20.907
Global hunger.

37
00:02:21.917 --> 00:02:25.576
Global hunger means the widespread lack of enough food around the world.

38
00:02:26.183 --> 00:02:28.798
Global hunger increased after the wars.

39
00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:32.488
Your turn. Say: Global hunger.

40
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:36.625
Global hunger.

41
00:02:37.383 --> 00:02:38.884
Climate resilience.

42
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:44.484
Climate resilience means the ability to recover from the shocks of changing weather.

43
00:02:45.083 --> 00:02:48.209
Climate resilience protects small farms from floods.

44
00:02:48.817 --> 00:02:52.073
Your turn. Say: Climate resilience.

45
00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:56.341
Climate resilience.

46
00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:01.214
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:08.801
Visal, an article says 'Farmers thought the rains would return, but the drought was to last three years.' What is 'was to last'?

48
00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:20.699
It is the future in the past: a past view of what came later. We use would, was going to or was to. 'Was to' often shows events we now know happened afterwards.

49
00:03:21.783 --> 00:03:24.340
What is the difference between would and was going to?

50
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:36.870
'Was going to' shows an earlier plan or sign: 'Aid was going to arrive, but the roads flooded.' 'Would' shows a past prediction: 'Experts warned prices would rise.'

51
00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:39.774
Why is it useful in reporting?

52
00:03:40.883 --> 00:03:52.095
It lets journalists describe what people expected at a past moment, often followed by 'but'. 'Harvests were due to improve, but the crop yield fell.'

53
00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:54.710
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.999
Grammar focus: Future in the past: would and was going to and was to.

55
00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:01.521
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:02.217 --> 00:04:06.085
Experts warned that food prices would rise after the harvest failed.

57
00:04:06.983 --> 00:04:09.703
Aid was going to arrive, but the roads flooded.

58
00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:12.988
The drought was to last for three more years.

59
00:04:13.933 --> 00:04:22.832
Use would, was going to, was due to or was to for the future seen from a past moment: Experts said prices would rise. Often followed by but.

60
00:04:23.583 --> 00:04:27.083
Be careful! Don't say: He said he will arrive late.

61
00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:31.671
Say: He said he would arrive late.

62
00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:34.261
Use would for the future in the past.

63
00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:37.731
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:38.533 --> 00:04:39.906
Feeding a Growing World.

65
00:04:40.206 --> 00:04:45.468
Food insecurity affects hundreds of millions of people, and global hunger remains stubborn.

66
00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:51.566
Farmers hoped the next crop yield would be larger, but a long drought was to destroy the harvest.

67
00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:57.747
Aid agencies said famine relief was going to arrive by sea, but conflict closed the ports.

68
00:04:58.733 --> 00:05:04.785
Sustainable agriculture and a stronger food supply chain give communities climate resilience.

69
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:12.095
Fairer agricultural trade, supported by good policies, would make food more available when harvests fail.

70
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.074
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:21.600
In 2019 analysts said that prices blank rise.

72
00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.089
A, will. B, would. C, are.

73
00:05:28.633 --> 00:05:30.482
The answer is B: would.

74
00:05:31.617 --> 00:05:34.661
The amount of food produced from a field is the crop blank.

75
00:05:34.961 --> 00:05:38.022
A, yield. B, debt. C, treaty.

76
00:05:41.567 --> 00:05:43.427
The answer is A: yield.

77
00:05:44.583 --> 00:05:47.697
Choose the sentence that expresses the future in the past.

78
00:05:47.997 --> 00:05:55.122
A, Prices will rise next year.. B, They said prices would rise the next year.. C, Prices rise next year..

79
00:05:58.667 --> 00:06:01.978
The answer is B: They said prices would rise the next year..

80
00:06:03.117 --> 00:06:06.846
The ability of farms to recover from climate shocks is climate blank.

81
00:06:07.146 --> 00:06:10.869
A, resilience. B, relief. C, trade.

82
00:06:14.433 --> 00:06:16.479
The answer is A: resilience.

83
00:06:17.617 --> 00:06:21.671
According to the reading, why did aid fail to arrive by sea?

84
00:06:21.971 --> 00:06:27.691
A, conflict closed the ports. B, the harvest was large. C, prices fell.

85
00:06:31.233 --> 00:06:34.220
The answer is A: conflict closed the ports.

86
00:06:35.433 --> 00:06:38.538
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:39.383 --> 00:06:43.589
How might climate change threaten global food security in the coming decades?

88
00:06:52.383 --> 00:06:53.802
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:54.102 --> 00:07:20.967
Climate change may threaten food security through longer droughts, floods and heat waves that reduce crop yield. Rising temperatures can also spread pests and damage fisheries. As harvests fail, prices rise and the poorest households suffer first. Sustainable agriculture, stronger food supply chains and fair agricultural trade can build climate resilience.

90
00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:22.999
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:23.883 --> 00:07:30.158
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future in the past: would and was going to and was to.

92
00:07:30.458 --> 00:07:35.258
Today's value is food responsibility. Value food and the people who produce it.

93
00:07:35.558 --> 00:07:38.361
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:38.661 --> 00:07:41.627
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

